{"name":"Salt Glaze","hex":"#cfd4ce","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salt glaze","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glaze produced upon ceramic wares by putting common salt in the kilns after they have been fired."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salt_glaze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salt glaze pottery","blocks":[{"text":"Salt-glaze or salt glaze pottery is pottery, usually stoneware, with a ceramic glaze of glossy, translucent and slightly orange-peel-like texture which was formed by throwing common salt into the kiln during the higher temperature part of the firing process. Sodium from the salt reacts with silica in the clay body to form a glassy coating of sodium silicate. The glaze may be colourless or may be coloured various shades of brown, blue, or purple."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/The_Art_of_German_Stoneware_Philadelphia_2012_010.JPG/330px-The_Art_of_German_Stoneware_Philadelphia_2012_010.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_glaze_pottery"},{"title":"Salt","blocks":[{"text":"Salt is a mineral composed primarily of sodium chloride (NaCl). When used in food, especially in granulated form, it is more formally called table salt. In the form of a natural crystalline mineral, salt is also known as rock salt or halite. Salt is essential for life in general, and saltiness is one of the basic human tastes. Salt is one of the oldest and most ubiquitous food seasonings, and is known to uniformly improve the taste perception of food. Salting, brining, and pickling are ancient and important methods of food preservation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg/330px-Rock_salt_%28halitite%29_%28Billianwala_Salt_Member%2C_Salt_Range_Formation%2C_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian%3B_Khewra_Salt_Mine%2C_Salt_Range%2C_Pakistan%29_14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Salt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG/500px-Salt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Salt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG/1280px-Salt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Salt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG/500px-Salt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Salt glazed containers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalt_glazed_utilitarian_containers.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Salt_glaze_jug.JPG/500px-Salt_glaze_jug.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Salt_glaze_jug.JPG/1280px-Salt_glaze_jug.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Salt_glaze_jug.JPG/500px-Salt_glaze_jug.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Salt glaze jug, 19th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalt_glaze_jug.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Jug_with_Brown_Glaze_LACMA_M.2001.97.3.jpg/500px-Jug_with_Brown_Glaze_LACMA_M.2001.97.3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Jug_with_Brown_Glaze_LACMA_M.2001.97.3.jpg/1280p